code: 0
--- stdout
Use it once a release is tagged: it applies a verb to the issues the release
includes, as gh dojo <verb> 12 13 14 would. Before the tag, gh dojo release
main --dry-run shows what the next one holds; --dry-run lists each issue with
how it was found and its plan.

The release is the commits of <ref> (a tag, branch or commit as GitHub names
it: main, not origin/main; up to 1000) since the previous version tag: the
highest below <ref> when <ref> is one, else the highest; prereleases
(v1.2.3-rc.1) only for a prerelease. --since names the start instead; the
first release needs it.

An issue is in it when a merged pull request of a commit closes it (squash,
merge and rebase alike), or a commit message names it after a [release]
keywords one, by default closes, fixes, resolves (owner/repo#N for another
repository). With [release] suffix = true, so does "(#12)" ending a message's
first line; else such an issue is left out with a warning. Code in a message
(`Refs #12`) does not count.

Listed and left out, not failed: an issue only reverted commits name, a number
that is no issue the token can see, an open issue the verb's from does not
allow (a later release naming it again picks it up), a closed one in a final
status, and --exclude. Any other closed issue is refused (exit code 2).
For a verb that finishes issues (done), a warning names the issues waiting
for a release (statuses.resolved, not final) it does not include, with the
command for those it includes after all.

Usage:
  gh dojo release <ref> --verb <verb> [flags]

Examples:
  gh dojo release main --verb done --dry-run
  gh dojo release v0.2.0 --verb done --dry-run
  gh dojo release v0.2.0 --verb done
  gh dojo release v0.2.0 --verb done --exclude acme/api#3
  gh dojo release v0.1.0 --since 4c40894 --verb done --comment "Released in v0.1.0."

Flags:
      --by string        value for {by} in the verb's comment, for every issue
      --comment string   text for {comment} in the verb's comment, for every issue
      --dry-run          list the issues, how each was found and its plan, without changing anything
      --exclude issue    an issue found to leave out, by a pull request or a message: 12, "#12", owner/repo#12 or its URL; repeatable, or comma-separated
  -h, --help             help for release
      --since string     where the release starts, a tag, branch or commit; default: the previous version tag
      --verb string      the verb to apply to the release's issues: any without a required --assignee, --milestone or --of
      --yes              the user agreed: for a verb that is the user's decision

Global flags: --jq, --json, --template (see gh dojo --help)
